<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Suraj Mal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1707–1763</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Maharaja Suraj Mal</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Suraj Mal (1707–1763) was the Jat ruler of Bharatpur who turned his family's holdings west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a> into a powerful kingdom. He built the fortress of Lohagarh at Bharatpur, took Agra in 1761 and profited from the weakness of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> defeat at Panipat. He was killed in battle against the Rohilla chief Najib ad-Dawla near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> in 1763.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/suraj-mal.jpg" alt="Suraj Mal" width="320" height="464" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Raja_Surajmal_of_Bharatpur_(1707%E2%80%931763).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
